<aside><p>
	This marks my <em>2000th Post</em>! I was going to make a big deal of it,
	maybe look back over the years I've had this here website thingy,
	or post my favorite photos or something. But instead, I went hiking.
	Sue me!</p></aside>

<img src='20230510_000000.jpg' />

<p id='Extract'>South Mountain Park is more than just a recreational area; 
	it's a significant preservation of desert wilderness in the heart of Phoenix, 
	providing an essential refuge for both wildlife and people. And today
	I went car-hiking through part of it. (Car-hiking, it's a thing right?
	I mean, if you can car-camp, why not car-hike?)</p>

<video autoplay='autoplay' loop='loop'>
	<source src='20230510_000001.mp4' />
</video>

<p>South Mountain Park in Phoenix, Arizona, has a history that dates back to the 
	early 20th century. Its foundation is deeply tied to the development and 
	growth of Phoenix as a city. The initial acquisition of land for South 
	Mountain Park began in 1924 when the State of Arizona itself was only twelve years old.
	Phoenix's city commissioners, influenced by that era's national City Beautiful 
	movement, recognized the need for preserving natural desert landscapes for 
	public enjoyment. The movement emphasized the importance of including natural 
	beauty in urban planning, which was a revolutionary concept at the time.
	But everyone thought it was a good idea, since there was no conservative media
	to scream, <q><i>Socialism!!!</i></q></p>

<img src='20240510_112254.jpg' />

<p>Though it was more remote in 1924, paved roads and overlooks (some with restrooms)
	make it thoroughly accessible, even to someone like me who's 73 and has a bad hip.
	For those with better hips, there are also numerous trails, as you'll see in some of
	the photos below.</p>

<img src='20240510_112358.jpg' />

<p>The park has two or three entrances. The one I took was right off Central Avenue, to 
	Telegraph Pass Road.</p>

<img src='20240510_112853.jpg' />

<p>The saguaros were in bloom, as they are this time of year.
	Saguaros (Carnegiea gigantea) are cacti. They are one of the most iconic species of the 
	Sonoran Desert and are known for their tall, columnar growth and impressive lifespan, 
	which can exceed 150 years.</p>

<img src='20240510_113113.jpg' />

<p>The saguaro cactus blooms in late spring to early summer, usually from late April 
	through June, depending on the local climate conditions. The flowers appear on the 
	tips of the arms and the main trunk, opening at night and closing by the next midday.
	(I took these photos shortly before noon.)
	These large, white flowers are pollinated primarily by bats, as well as by birds and 
	bees during the daytime. Following successful pollination, the flowers develop into red, 
	fleshy fruits that are an important food source for wildlife during the hot desert summer.</p>

<img src='20240510_113451.jpg' />

<p>The view of Phoenix from South Mountain is truly spectacular and offers a unique 
	perspective on the sprawling metropolitan area nestled in the Valley of the Sun. 
	From various vantage points along the park�s trails, visitors can enjoy breathtaking 
	panoramic views. And note that, in taking these photos, I haven't yet even stepped
	out of my Tesla!</p>

<img src='20240510_113632.jpg' />

<p>The above photo, taken with my camera at 10x normal lens, shows a plane coming in
	for a landing at Sky Harbor Airport. (Okay, I may have gotten out of the car for that one!)</p>

<img src='20240510_113838.jpg' />

<p>South Mountain is part of a larger geological feature known as the Phoenix Mountains, 
	which includes several ranges and isolated peaks. The space between them (there's also
	a North Mountain) is what we locals call <q>The Valley of the Sun</q> or simply, <q>The Valley</q>,
	even though it's not a true valley, which is a geologic feature carved by a river.</p>

<img src='20240510_113926.jpg' />

<p>The formation of South Mountain, like other mountain ranges in the region, is primarily 
	attributed to tectonic activity, specifically crustal extension that occurred during 
	the Miocene epoch, roughly 25 to 5 million years ago.</p>

<img src='20240510_114003.jpg' />

<p>At the summit of South Mountain, there is a notable cluster of radio and television 
	antennae, which play a crucial role in broadcasting services across the Phoenix area. 
	This cluster includes various towers for FM radio and television stations, essential 
	for ensuring that communications cover the sprawling metropolitan area effectively. 
	The presence of these antennae at such a high elevation is strategic, leveraging the 
	mountain�s altitude to maximize broadcast range and clarity. This is also where cell
	phone signals are provided to practically the whole city.</p>

<img src='20240510_114248.jpg' />

<p>The saguaro's ability to flower and fruit annually is vital for the survival of many 
	desert species, making it a keystone species in its ecosystem. Despite its rugged 
	appearance, the saguaro is quite sensitive to its environment, requiring specific 
	conditions that are only found in the Sonoran Desert, spanning areas of Arizona, 
	California, and parts of Mexico.</p>

<img src='20240510_114307.jpg' />

<p>The saguaro cactus, with its towering, columnar structure, serves as a vital ecological 
	resource in the Sonoran Desert, particularly as a nesting site for various bird species. 
	Notably, the Gila woodpecker and the gilded flicker create nesting cavities within the 
	saguaro's flesh. These birds carve out holes with their beaks, and the cactus responds 
	by forming a callus tissue around the wound, which hardens to form a protective <q>boot</q> 
	around the nest. This adaptation prevents the cactus from losing too much moisture and 
	provides a safe haven for the birds� eggs and chicks.</p>

<img src='20240510_114508.jpg' />

<p>The ancestors of today's Akimel O'odham (Pima) and the Tohono O'odham peoples are
	called <q>Hohokam</q> in their language, which means <q>those who have gone</q>.
	It's used by archaeologists and historians to refer to the culture that inhabited 
	the Sonoran Desert, including South Mountain, from approximately CE 1 to about 1450.
	They left behind petroglyphs on some rocks that can be seen from the trail.</p>

<img src='20240510_115217.jpg' />

<p>Of course, the white guys in 1924 weren't really concerned, or possibly even cognizant,
	of the cultural significance to the O'oodham peoples of various sites and artifacts
	located in the park area. However, surrounding them with a park has, ironically,
	protected them; as, surely, the entire mountain would be now covered with condos if
	they hadn't.</p>

<img src='20240510_115421.jpg' />

<p>The above and next several photos were taken from and of Dobbins Lookout, often 
	referred to as Dobbins Point. This is the highest accessible point in South Mountain Park, 
	located at an elevation of 2,330 feet. It provides one of the most stunning panoramic 
	views of the Phoenix metropolitan area and its surrounding desert landscape. Visitors 
	can reach Dobbins Lookout by hiking, biking, or driving up the winding Summit Road, 
	which offers its own scenic vistas along the way.</p>

<img src='20240510_115449.jpg' />

<p>The area around Dobbins Lookout includes a stone ramada, built in the 1930s by the 
	Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC). This historic structure provides shade and a 
	place for visitors to rest and enjoy the scenery. It represents a piece of the 
	park's history, reflecting the era's craftsmanship and the role of New Deal programs 
	in developing public lands for recreational use.</p>

<img src='20240510_115650.jpg' />

<p>The lookout is named after Robert Lee Dobbins, a local entrepreneur and early advocate 
	for the preservation of South Mountain as a public park. In recognition of his efforts, 
	this particular point was dedicated to him, underscoring the area's historical 
	significance and the community's appreciation for natural conservation.</p>

<img src='20240510_115801.jpg' />

<p>And, by the way&mdash;entrance to the park is free of charge!</p>

<img src='20240510_115909.jpg' />
<img src='20240510_121533.jpg' />

<p>From Dobbins Lookout, visitors have a clear view that stretches across the 
	urban expanse below, from the skyscrapers of downtown Phoenix to the rugged 
	outlines of distant mountains.</p>

<img src='20240510_121625.jpg' />
<img src='20240510_121702.jpg' />

<p>The next lookout I stopped at was called Buena Vista. It featured a beautifull-positioned
	bench from which one could sit and meditate, I suppose.</p>

<div class="Carousel">
	<img src='20240510_121707.jpg' />
	<img src='20240510_121720.jpg' />
	<img src='20240510_121730.jpg' />
	<img src='20240510_121813.jpg' />
	<img src='20240510_121836.jpg' />
</div>

<p>As one drives along the <i>very</i> twisty road, every few minutes a new, spectactular
	view of the city is revealed. This is probably why the speed limit is 25mph, with a
	strong caution to limit speed to 15mph on curves.</p>

<p>The whole road is curves.</p>

<img src='20240510_121902.jpg' />

<p>My last visit today was to Gila Valley Lookout, which is on the south side of
	South Mountain and, therefore, looking <i>away</i> from the city.</p>

<img src='20240510_123023.jpg' />

<p>Positioned to provide stunning vistas over the Gila Valley and beyond, this lookout is a 
	treasure for those seeking a more serene and contemplative experience,
	as well as those who become aroused at the sight of endless rows of cookie-cutter
	houses.</p>

<img src='20240510_123054.jpg' />

<p>Gila Valley Lookout is also located quite close to the array of towers
	at the summit. It's at once ghastly and compelling. While these structures 
	are crucial for communication, they also pose challenges, such as visual 
	pollution and potential environmental impacts. Managing these impacts while 
	maintaining the area's natural and recreational value is a continual 
	challenge for park authorities and city planners. This juxtaposition of 
	technology and nature at South Mountain really provides a compelling narrative 
	about modern life's complexities in a desert city, and the need to balance
	the human requirements for today against our need to have a habitable
	world in the future.</p>

<img src='20240510_123601.jpg' />

<p>Look at the rock above. See how it's basically dark, but lighter where pieces have broken
	off? Well, that's called desert varnish, a dark, often shiny coating found on the 
	surface of rocks in arid environments like deserts. This intriguing phenomenon 
	has captivated the interest of geologists and archaeologists because of its appearance 
	and the processes involved in its formation. Desert varnish is composed primarily
	of clay minerals mixed with iron and manganese oxides. The iron oxides give the 
	varnish a reddish-brown color, while manganese oxides contribute a darker, black layer.
	The exact composition can vary depending on the local environment and the minerals 
	present in the area.</p>

<img src="20240510_123602.jpg" />

<p>Desert varnish often holds significant archaeological value because ancient peoples 
	used these varnished surfaces as canvases for petroglyphs. They created rock art by 
	scraping away the dark varnish to reveal the lighter rock beneath. This contrast in 
	color made the designs highly visible and durable, preserving cultural expressions 
	that are still visible today. </p>

<img src='20240510_124241.jpg' />

<p>This is not a loop road, so at Gila Valley Lookout I had to return the way I came.
	And, being a 73-year-old with a bladder the size of a lentil, I needed to stop again at Dobbins,
	which has a restroom.</p>

<img src='20240510_125148.jpg' />
<img src='20240510_125204.jpg' />

<p>There are a number of <q>little</q> lookouts as well as the larger ones.</p>

<img src='20240510_125232.jpg' />
<img src='20240510_125300.jpg' />
<img src='20240510_125730.jpg' />

<p>And then, just before exiting the park, I got to see a bird having lunch
	at the saguaro's flower bar!</p>

<video autoplay='autoplay' loop='loop'>
	<source src='20240510_125945.mp4' />
</video>

<p>There are (at least) two other entrances to the park, whose roads do not connect
	with Telegraph Pass Road. (I'll bet you can guess the history behind that name!)
	It's been almost five years since my last visit; hopefully I'll make it back
	sooner than that to check out the other two!</p>

